E("es-u3-330-q01",3,["EA12.3.30"],1,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("In which environment does limestone, a biochemical sedimentary rock, most commonly form?","Warm, shallow marine water where organisms with shells and skeletons thrive",["In fast-flowing mountain streams carrying gravel and sand down from high ground","In hot springs on land, where mineral-rich water rises and cools at the surface","On the deep floors of cold oceans, far below the reach of sunlight and waves"],"Most limestone accumulates in warm, shallow seas, where shell-bearing organisms are abundant and their calcite remains pile up after death.",{});}]),["Biochemical rocks need abundant life to supply the material."]);
E("es-u3-330-q02",3,["EA12.3.30"],2,"var",varset([function(){return mcq("Why are swamps the characteristic environment for the biochemical rock coal?","Swamp water is stagnant and low in oxygen, so dead plant material accumulates instead of decaying completely.",["Swamp water is rich in dissolved calcium carbonate that precipitates around plants.","Swamps are flooded by seawater that preserves the plants in salt.","Swamp plants contain silica that turns directly into coal."],"In an oxygen-poor swamp, plant debris is buried faster than decay can destroy it. That accumulation is the first step in forming coal, a biochemical (organic) sedimentary rock.",{});}]),["The key is what does not happen to the dead plants."]);

